# The binomial expansions, each a universal ring identity over the integers, proved by the commutative-ring normalizer
# and checked by `seed test`. The binomial theorem expands a power of a sum as a sum of terms weighted by the binomial
# coefficients, the entries of Pascal's triangle. The low cases are the workhorses of algebra; here are the fourth and
# fifth powers and the factorization of a fourth-power difference, complementing the squares and cubes in the polynomial
# identity file.

# The fourth power of a sum: (a + b)^4 = a^4 + 4 a^3 b + 6 a^2 b^2 + 4 a b^3 + b^4, the coefficients 1 4 6 4 1.
rule fourth-power-of-sum-expands-by-pascal-s-row-1-4-6-4-1
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call multiply
          call add
            read a
            read b
          call add
            read a
            read b
        call multiply
          call add
            read a
            read b
          call add
            read a
            read b
      call add
        call add
          call add
            call add
              call multiply
                call multiply
                  call multiply
                    read a
                    read a
                  read a
                read a
              call multiply
                code 4
                call multiply
                  call multiply
                    read a
                    read a
                  call multiply
                    read a
                    read b
            call multiply
              code 6
              call multiply
                call multiply
                  read a
                  read a
                call multiply
                  read b
                  read b
          call multiply
            code 4
            call multiply
              read a
              call multiply
                call multiply
                  read b
                  read b
                read b
        call multiply
          call multiply
            call multiply
              read b
              read b
            read b
          read b

# The difference of fourth powers factors fully: a^4 - b^4 = (a - b)(a + b)(a^2 + b^2). Two steps of the difference of
# squares.
rule difference-of-fourth-powers-factors-into-three-terms
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call subtract
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read a
            read a
          call multiply
            read a
            read a
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read b
            read b
          call multiply
            read b
            read b
      call multiply
        call multiply
          call subtract
            read a
            read b
          call add
            read a
            read b
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read a
          call multiply
            read b
            read b

# Pascal's recurrence at row four: each interior coefficient is the sum of the two above it, 6 = 4 + ... no, the rule is
# C(4,2) = C(3,1) + C(3,2) = 3 + 3 = 6. A false coefficient would break the sum.
hold pascal-s-recurrence-builds-central-fourth-row-coefficient
  call is-equal
    call add
      code 3
      code 3
    code 6
